{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785006740,"nonce":"PUJz5ZeZBNMMgzU5v2LqU7EEiTM=","title":"**Deferred from b48df22** (PR #82), which shipped Forgejo onboarding without ever touching a live Forgejo.","message":"Everything in the Forgejo path is asserted against unit tests of the request/response shapes we *believe* Forgejo uses. That is thin evidence, and this slice already produced one bug that was exactly a wrong guess about the Forgejo API: `register_webhook` sent no `config.secret`, so every delivery from a Scarab-registered hook would have been rejected 401 by our own endpoint — registration reporting success while nothing ever ran.\n\nThe same class of guess is still unverified in three places:\n\n- **`list_accessible_repos`** — the `/user/repos` response shape and its pagination (the bind pick-list depends on it).\n- **`config.secret` on hook creation** — whether a real Forgejo accepts the field and then signs deliveries with it.\n- **The push payload spelling** — normalization assumes `owner.username`, which GitHub never sends; asserted from docs, not from a delivery.\n\n**What to do:** stand up a real Forgejo (container is fine), point a Scarab at it, and drive the flow the acceptance test simulates — add connection → pick-list → bind → hook registered → push → run. The `SCARAB_TEST_KUBE`-style env-gated tier is the right home so it stays out of the default suite.\n\n**Acceptance**\n- [ ] Against a real Forgejo: connection create, `available-repos`, bind, `register_webhook` all succeed.\n- [ ] A real push produces a signed delivery that `/webhooks/forgejo` accepts and turns into a Run.\n- [ ] Any shape mismatch found is fixed in `scarab-forge-forgejo` with a unit test pinning the real shape.\n- [ ] Gated so CI does not require a Forgejo.","files":null}]}